Matthew C Roberts (therobertsDELETETHESELETTERS@sprintmail.com) wrote:
: combination, depending on your exact application. Using the Compaq compilers
: for Alpha Linux (free for "enthusiasts") will reduce this difference, but
: certainly not erase it 

The typical performance difference between otherwise identical systems
running Tru64 and Linux, with identical application binaries from CPQ 
compilers, is between 0 and 15% [*]. Most of the difference is due to
the lack of page colouring in stock Linux kernels. With experimental
page-colouring patches, Linux was reported to run Tru64 binaries at
essentially the same speed as Tru64. See archives of the Beowulf mailing
list at http://www.beowulf.org/ for the information on the page colouring
patches, and other useful additions for high-performance computing 
applications of Linux.

I certainly have nothing against Tru64 (heck, I run almost hundred Tru64
systems, and am pretty happy with them), but the performance advantage
of Tru64 over Linux/alpha has been steadily diminishing over the last
three years, up to the point where the choice between the two is now
driven mostly by the factors other than computational performance. IMHO,
naturally.

/Serge.P

[*] In tight memory configurations, Linux may actually turn out to be 
substantially faster, by virtue of having a smaller kernel, and leaving
more memory for your applications.

