JJ (john.jemiolo@mindspring.com) wrote:
: Tru64 supports more hardware than Linux on  "Alpha"   I'm not comparing it
: to Linux Intel.

Very well. I am not aware of an exhaustive list of supported hardware on
Linux/alpha. Still, examination of the kernel source tree shows that 
essentially all PCI scsi, ethernet, and audio controllers supported by 
Linux/intel are also supported by Linux/alpha. Same goes for most PCMCIA
and ISA cards. http://talisman.alphalinux.org/ALOHcl/ and 
http://linuxhardware.net/ give (an incomplete) list of hardware reported 
to work under Linux.

I am not aware of a master list of hardware supported by Tru64, either.
However, a good idea of the supported hardware can be obtained from Tru64
release notes, and from online system configurator. Neither resource gives
a complete list of the supported cards (Tru64 does support a few older,
retired cards "unofficially"), but it does give a fair idea of the possible
choices. Usually, only a very few cards in each category are supported.
Note that even when there is a CPQ-supported card, and presumably identical
third-party card, third-party cards often won't work with Tru64, as CPQ 
loves to use modified firmware for their cards.

Release notes are at (this should be a single line):
http://tru64unix.compaq.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/V51_HTML/ARH8WBTE/TITLE.HTM)

Configurator is at: http://www5.compaq.com/alphaserver/configure.html

Now, Tru64 does support Turbochannel systems, which are not supported
at all by Linux. Since you can no longer buy these systems new, and
their performance is very far from stellar by now, this is pretty much 
irrelevant.

/Serge.P

