Serguei Patchkovskii wrote:

> Vijay Gupta (vgupta@mit.edu) wrote:
> : Is it worth paying $5K to go for True Unix 64 ?
>
> Depending on what you are going to use this box for, you may qualify
> for $100 educational and hobbyist licence for Tru64. (Unfortunately,
> I cannot give you the URL, since our off-campus connectivity seems
> to be down presently). If you are careful with the hardware you put
> in the box (Tru64 is very choosy wrt SCSI controllers and video
> cards), your "Linux" system should be able to run Tru64.
>
> In any event, US$5k price differential between a Linux and Tru64
> system sound awfully large. The last time I checked, workstation-
> class Tru64 licences were "only" about US$1k list - and you were
> normally able to negotiate a substantial discount. You are probably
> being quited a bare-bones Linux system, with $15 video card, and
> an IDE drive, against a loaded Tru64 box, with an overpriced $1k
> video card, overpriced CPQ-branded $2k SCSI drive, and various
> other goodies ...
>
> /Serge.P
>
> ---
> Home page: http://www.cobalt.chem.ucalgary.ca/ps/

Actually, $5k sounds about right.  You pay about $1k for the True64 UNIX
License, another ~ $1-2K for SMP/Per Processor License (note that he
said a dual-Processor system), another $1-2K for True64 UNIX C Compiler
(although I think you can ask for GCC), and another $1-2K for
FORTRAN Compiler.  COMPAQ rakes you coming and going on True64 UNIX
Licenses.  And this does not even get you into the Networking aspect;
this is only a 2-user license.

Regarding the Hobbyist License, note that SMP support is turned OFF for
the Hobbyist License.

I checked into all this when purchasing my UP2000 and also found that
you also pay a higher price for your SCSI Controller on account of the
True64 UNIX Driver; something like $500.  (I ended up just getting
LINUX only.)  With COMPAQ releasing their True64 UNIX C, C++, and
FORTRAN Compilers for LINUX and available for download, you may not need
True64 UNIX.  Check http://www.alphalinux.org for more info on COMPAQ's
Compilers.

GFX Card is probably an 8 MB ELSA Gloria Synergy Card.

HTH.

Daniel




