Have you tried this?

It doesn't seem to make sense to me.  Why the ~ in front of user?  Is that a special
folder somebody created, or is it part of their login name, or a type-o, or
something I don't understand?
Also, on my system, in order for httpd to reach the /home/joeuser/public_html/*
files, all of those paths needed to be read and execute permissions, in other words,
755.

Here's another idea:
In the apache configuration files somewhere, there must be a setting that tells it
"When you get a request for http://www.domain.com/~user, look in the
/home/user/public_html folder," because not every system puts the users under /home.

So that setting must be changeable, for example, to /home/personalpages/user.
If the users want the public_html folder in their regular /home/user folder, make a
softlink.

Allow the full 755 permissions on /home/personalpages, because if it's public anyway
(accessible through http://www.domain.com/~user) then who will be upset that
somebody read it?  And you still can preserve normal permissions on your /home/user
folder.




