In comp.os.linux.alpha Marble Head <Marble.Head@nospam.guacamole.com> wrote:

>> cd /home
>> chmod 711 ~user
>> chmod 755 ~user/public_html
>> chmod 644 ~user/public_html/<files/here>
>> chmod 755 ~user/public_html/<dirs/here>

> Have you tried this?

> It doesn't seem to make sense to me.  Why the ~ in front of user?  
> Is that a special folder somebody created, or is it part of 
> their login name, or a type-o, or something I don't understand?


 It's something you don't understand.  It's also pretty basic
 UNIX/Linux so it is generally assumed that users will understand
 it.  From most (almost all) UNIX shells (command line interpreters/
 interfaces) the ~ (tilde or "twiddle") is an abbreviation for
 "the home directory of."  So ~/foo is the "foo" file or directory
 under (the current user's) home directory.  ~foo/bar is "the 
 bar file or directory under the home directory of the user named
 "foo."  (Note that "foo" and "bar" are traditional "meta variables"
 --- the are used in examples just the way that you math teacher 
 might have used "a" and "b").

 In common URL usage the ~ has a similar meaning.  The NCSA 
 Apache and many other web servers are configured to take a 
 URL of the form ~foo (as in http://somewhere.not/~foo/bar.html)
 and expand it to "the public_html directory under the home
 directory of user foo."  This is a slightly different but
 rather "intuitive" interpretation to people who are used to
 the common UNIX meaning of the ~.  Considering  that the 
 web and the Inernet were primarily developed by UNIX programmers
 and users this makes alot of sense.

 It's possible that your web server may be configured to 
 treat ~ characters in URLs differently.  For example I usually 
 configure my systems to use ~*/.web/ instead of ~*/public_html/
 as "the user's web files."  That's because I prefer the shorter
 name and I don't need to see my "web" directory tree every time
 I do an ls in my home directory.  

 It's also possible that your web server might be configured
 to ignore the ~ completely.  Also note that it is technically
 not strictly conformant with the standards to put a ~ in a 
 URL --- it's supposed to be encoded (%7E??) though it's quite
 rare in practice.

> Also, on my system, in order for httpd to reach the 
> /home/joeuser/public_html/* files, all of those paths needed 
> to be read and execute permissions, in other words, 755.

 Should read:  "those paths need to be 'world accessible'..."

 Technically you just have to make sure that your 
 public_html directory is accessible to the web server.  The
 web server should be running under its own special user ID
 (often named "web").  You might be able to accomplish this 
 with just group permissions (mode 750 on public_html and
 possibly on your home directory as well).  However, it's 
 usually pretty unlikely that you'd want to serve web pages
 from a "public_html" directory that you would want to keep
 confidential from other local users.  It's possible to do 
 that sort of think using CGI programs and the Apache suExec
 module if you really need to.

> Here's another idea:
> In the apache configuration files somewhere, there must be a 
> setting that tells it "When you get a request for 
> http://www.domain.com/~user, look in the /home/user/public_html 
> folder," because not every system puts the users under /home.

 It is correct that there is such a setting.  However, that is 
 completely indepedent of the fact that some users might have
 home directories that are not under /home.  

 The normal UNIX mechanism for looking up a user's home 
 directory (and for matching numeric UIDs to user names, and
 for looking up other "locally public" information about any UNIX
 account is to use the getpwent() library functions.  This
 could provide a directory name under /home for one user and
 a directory under /usr/share for another, etc.  

 The getpwent() function traditionally looked through the 
 /etc/passwd file.  On modern versions of UNIX it might 
 look up information via a set of NIS maps, it might access
 a set of dbm "hash/index" files, or it might use other 
 mechanisms.  On a GNU/Linux or other GNU system (one using 
 the GNU glibc 2.x libraries) it might involve the library
 loading various .so (shared object) files and querying 
 any sort of back-end directory service (LDAP, Hesiod, etc).

 So, if the ~ feature of your web server is enabled it is 
 not likely to be doing a simple string replacement of 
 ~ with something like /home/.../public_html/ --- it's almost
 certainly doing a proper getpwent() and then appending the
 "public_html" or other directory name to the end of that.

> So that setting must be changeable, for example, to 
> /home/personalpages/user.  If the users want the public_html 
> folder in their regular /home/user folder, make a softlink.

 The web server might be configured not to follow symbolic links.

> Allow the full 755 permissions on /home/personalpages, because 
> if it's public anyway (accessible through http://www.domain.com/~user) 
> then who will be upset that somebody read it?  And you still can 
> preserve normal permissions on your /home/user folder.

 Actually it is possible (if one was using .htaccess) that one
 might want to keep "confidential" data under a ~/public_html/
 directory tree.  One news poster recently posed a question
 (I don't remember where) that described a set of patches to
 their web server that caused the web server to adopt the 
 same effective user ID as the owner of a file/directory that
 was listed in the URL.  This allegedly provided the desired
 level of additional security (although it seems quite risky
 since it might have had its own bugs that make the whole
 server MUCH more dangerous; I'd recommend a mechanism where
 the primary web server could "hand off" any ~foo/ URL requests
 to a different process (file descriptors, including those
 bound to internet domain sockets can be passed via UNIX domain 
 sockets) and have each user that wanted this sort of 
 feature running a custom process (probably running with a 
 specific supplemental group) --- that should be much safer).




