On Thu, 21 Sep 2000, Marble Head wrote:

> > cd /home
> > chmod 711 ~user
> > chmod 755 ~user/public_html
> > chmod 644 ~user/public_html/<files/here>
> > chmod 755 ~user/public_html/<dirs/here>
> 
> Have you tried this?
Yep, it works. Believe him.

> It doesn't seem to make sense to me.  Why the ~ in front of user?  Is that a special
> folder somebody created, or is it part of their login name, or a type-o, or
> something I don't understand?
The latter. The "~" character is an abbreviation for /home/, hence ~user
means /home/user.

> Also, on my system, in order for httpd to reach the /home/joeuser/public_html/*
> files, all of those paths needed to be read and execute permissions, in other words,
> 755.
No, that is wrong. In order to reach public_html, /home/user only needs to
have the "executable/dir-access" flag set (1). Apache does not search for
the public_html directory, but changes to it assuming it is there. That's
the difference. However, if another user wants to "ls" the contents of
/home/user, then the read flag (4) must be set.

I hope this helps. If you have doubts about this, try "man chmod".

Greetings,

Gunnar

