use that to.  It was DESIGNED to do that.  Plus, they offer excellent
customer support.  And I do mean excellent.  Even before they were backed by
BSDi they were good.  And, you can use SCO Unix, Solaris, SVR4, and Linux
binaries.  And you don't need any user licenses. Free fast and basically
bulletproof (as long as you cron like you're supposed to and watch your
logs) FreeBSD is one bad motor scooter.  It is my personaly favorite.

Next in my pick would be SuSE Linux.  SuSE is like the industrial strength
version of Linux.  It flat out gets with the program on Alphas.  Plus it can
readily accept Debian, Red Hat, and various other distribution packages.  In
a word - smokin'.  This is an EXCELLENT distribution.  And I've noticed it
doesn't have anywhere near the amount of patches and security holes/fixes
that Red Hat does.  If you are a Linux freak then this is one I'd get.  I
like this flavor of Linux - A LOT!!!

Then you have the tried and ture Red Hat.  Used by millions and cussed at by
just as many, this takes a back seat to SuSE becasue on an install level it
is much, much easier and it is more stable than Red Hat.  It takes a lot to
tune and tweak a RH distribution.  But once you get it right, it's right.
It might be high maintenance, but it is also hi-performance.

Lemme put it this way.  In the Alpha world I see it like this:

1 FreeBSD - The bad boy.  Finicky yes, but once right it is like a 426
Street Hemi with twin AFBs and a 4 speed in a '68 Road Runner.  Put
simply, one bad ass machine.

2 SuSE - A '67 Shelby 'Stang with the 428 SCJ with dual Holley 4 barrels and
 a Top Loader.  Another bad ass.

3 Red Hat - A built Small Block Chevy in your garden variety Nova or Camaro.
Just remember to keep the oil changed and the plugs fresh because it is a
high maintenance and tempermental rod that falls into the "dime-a-dozen"
category.
And by the way, in Spanish Nova means something that doesn't "go" and Camaro
roughly equates to loose bowels (the trots).  Sounds about right...

Now, I don't mean to flame anybody.  That is just my opinion.  You asked, I
tell.  But before you get anything from FTP or buy a distribution, just make
sure that your box is supported by the distribution.  I am fairly drooling
and watching my mail box for the newest FreeBSD Alpha relaese, and I've
heard nothing but good from people who are doing the SuSE thing.  I get
half-n-half on RH.  I only know one person who does Debian but he's got a
bad case of tunnel vision, So all I get from goof ball is praise.  It could
blow out his processor and he'd still think it's the greatest thing since
peanut butter.  And, I never really have used any other type of
distribution.

Hope this helped ya!

Hack

