Yup.  SuSE or Red Hat will do the job.  The EGCS stuff will do what you need
it to do.  It's ANSI compliant anyway.  All of that will put you in the
32-bit realm.  It'll allow you to do long and double long computations with
no problems.  But, it is 32-bit.  Now if you want to go the 64-bit route
then you only have one option.  And that is FreeBSD.  When booting from SRM
it puts FreeBSD in 64-bit mode, and the compilers are also ANSI compliant.

So if you want Linux, I'd suggest SuSE first.  Then RH.  If you want UNIX
then get FreeBSD.  I wrote a little something about this earlier.  Just pul
a search from "Hack".

But before you do anything make sure that the OS supports the box you want
to use.  If not it is just wasted money all the way 'round.

Hack

