??? What do you mean by 32-bit? Linux/Alpha employs LP64 model 
even before FreeBSD/Alpha existed. The first 64-bit version was 
BLADE 0.2/0.3 from Digital Semiconductor with 1.3 kernel, which 
was made public in 1996(correct me if I'm wrong). 

Besides, "the" compiler of FreeBSD is nothing but GCC(or EGCS, 
if you insist on this name.) Linux/Alpha has Compaq C/C++/Fortran 
which are optimized for Alpha architecture. 

If you are in doubt, try the following program on Linux/Alpha 
to see it's a 64-bit OS:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <limits.h>

int main(void)
{
    printf("sizeof pointer: %d bits\n", sizeof(void *) * CHAR_BIT);
    printf("sizeof double : %d bits\n", sizeof(double) * CHAR_BIT);
    printf("sizeof float  : %d bits\n", sizeof(float *) * CHAR_BIT);
    printf("sizeof long   : %d bits\n", sizeof(long *) * CHAR_BIT);
    return 0;
}

