It just means some naive programmers believing double and float 
are both 32-bits will get upset when working on Cray T3E. Compilers 
should issue a lot of warning messages on implicit type casts. 

Again, Linux/Alpha is a true 64-bit operating system, 
in the sense its address space is 64-bit. Current implementation 
allows a user space of 2^42 bytes and it seems to be a limitation 
imposed by PAL code. I'm no expert on computer, so I leave the 
64-bitness issue to other more knowledgeable people. 

