Float is 32 bits on any alpha and double is 64.  There are IEEE
standards for these things...

What confuses Cray programmers the first time is that on the big
vector machines, 'single' precision is 64 bits and 'double' precision
is 128.

The big advantage to using float type on an alpha (if you can afford
the loss of precision) is that the cache line holds twice as many
'numbers' which is quite often the limiting factor in performance,
i.e. 'how much data can we get pumped through the bus to the processor'.
But once the data is on the processor, doing calculations will
take just as much time with float or double.  They use the same
transistors to do either, they just fix up the leftover bits
with floating point calculations to come out to what they should be
according to IEEE rules.

Compilers should issue warnings about implicit type casts =-]

This has been a most confusing thread...

Regards,

Tony Hammitt


P.S. If you think this is strange, try thinking about the IBM
RS64 line of processors that can have 32 or 64 bit address space
threads running concurrently.  Some people never did understand
that it would execute 32 bit code just like always but could also
execute 64 bit code (like the OS) at the same time.  It even has
twinned libraries like a PowerMac.  It really is confusing..


