Hack wrote:
> 
> Yup.  SuSE or Red Hat will do the job.  The EGCS stuff will do what you need
> it to do.  It's ANSI compliant anyway.  All of that will put you in the
> 32-bit realm.  It'll allow you to do long and double long computations with
> no problems.  But, it is 32-bit.  Now if you want to go the 64-bit route
> then you only have one option.  And that is FreeBSD.  When booting from SRM
> it puts FreeBSD in 64-bit mode, and the compilers are also ANSI compliant.

I have my own sizeof program that I run on different platforms.  Here's
what it shows when I run it on my Alpha Linux system.  The first program
was compiled with gcc, and the second with ccc:

/home/richard/src > ./sizeof_tst.gcc 
char        1
short       2
int         4
long        8
long long   8
float       4
double      8
long double 8
void*       8
size_t      8
/home/richard/src > ./sizeof_tst.ccc 
char        1
short       2
int         4
long        8
long long   8
float       4
double      8
long double 8
void*       8
size_t      8

Here's the program:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
main(){
  printf( "char        %d\n", sizeof( char        ) );
  printf( "short       %d\n", sizeof( short       ) );
  printf( "int         %d\n", sizeof( int         ) );
  printf( "long        %d\n", sizeof( long        ) );
  printf( "long long   %d\n", sizeof( long long   ) );
  printf( "float       %d\n", sizeof( float       ) );
  printf( "double      %d\n", sizeof( double      ) );
  printf( "long double %d\n", sizeof( long double ) );
  printf( "void*       %d\n", sizeof( void*       ) );
  printf( "size_t      %d\n", sizeof( size_t      ) );
}

-- 

