Neoklis (neoklis@my-deja.com) wrote:
: 
: tow tries to compile. I have libc 2.1.2 and EGCS-2.91.66
: according to yast. (I get the feeling I am asking for too much!)

: Try 1

: <snipped lots of warnings>
: /tmp/cccTvhhV.s: Assembler messages:
: /tmp/cccTvhhV.s:3630: Error: unknown opcode `sqrttsu'
: /tmp/cccTvhhV.s:3700: Error: unknown opcode `sqrttsu'

Crash?  Where it is?  I do not see one.  This is not even a compiler
but, as it says above, assembler which is trying to process a compiler
output and runs into unknown opcodes. /usr/bin/as is a part of
'binutils' package.  Update to something more recent.

: make: *** [bootstrap] Error 2

Well, yes, so you got an error from that.  If an assembler is fed
things which it does not understand this is an _expected_ behaviour.

: Try 2

: <snipped lots of warnings>
: make[2]: [stage2-start] Error 1 (ignored)
: xgcc: Internal compiler error: program cc1 got fatal signal 11

'xgcc' is the second stage compiler created while trying to recompile
gcc.  If the first stage had problems but you still produced executables
(from empty object files, for example, which happens if you manage to
confuse a compiler) then anything can happen when you try to use it.
You can achieve that effect when you will restart a compilation of gcc
after a failure on the first try and without doing 'make clean' in
between (or, even better, 'make distclean').  Most likely you are seeing
effects of exactly this.

You will not get very far with that without decent 'binutils' first.

 Michal



